WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.750
Imagine a puzzle where the pieces fit
themselves together without human help.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:15.530
These special kinds of puzzles are
actually all around us, on a scale we

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.700
normally can't see, and they put
themselves together in a process called

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.900
self-assembly. Self-assembly occurs when
molecules spontaneously organize into

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.789
complex structures, like smart puzzle
pieces. They are directed to their proper

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:32.510
locations by attractions and repulsions
that result from their electrical

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:37.820
charges, magnetic properties, and
complementary shapes. This means that the

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.199
molecules can assemble spontaneously
into ordered rods, sheets, spheres and

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:45.559
other useful structures that are much
bigger than the individual building

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:50.120
blocks. One of these useful structures
self-assembles every time you wash your

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.180
hands. Soap is made up of molecules called

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.500
fatty acids. These molecules have a
hydrophilic end that's attracted to

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.699
water and a hydrophobic chain that's
attracted to oily substances like grease

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:06.229
and dirt. When you wash your hands, the
large hydrophobic chains cluster around

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:10.130
the particles of grease and dirt, leaving
the hydrophilic end sticking out,

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:16.159
surrounded by water. This self-assembled
dirt trap helps the water pilll grease

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:19.719
and dirt off your hands and leaves your
skin squeaky clean!

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:25.250
Remember that self-assembly organizes
materials at a very small scale. In fact,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.789
the molecules involved in self-assembly
are often measured in units of

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:33.289
nanometers or a billionth of a meter--
that's a 1000 times smaller than a

00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:38.329
human hair!
This makes self-assembly especially

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.380
useful for nanotechnologies, which are
found in everything from computer chips

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:49.340
to electric cars to medical technology.
Speaking of medical technology, one

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.939
example is self assembling action is a
new method for getting medicine to the

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:58.670
right places in sick patients, a field
known as drug delivery. Researchers have

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.299
found a way to make thin molecular
coatings self-assemble around small

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:07.009
doses of anti-cancer drugs, similar to
how soap molecules surround an oily

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:11.480
substance. These coatings help the
assembly bind selectively to cancer

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.290
cells and release most of the drug when
it gets to those cell,s where it can do

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.030
the most good.
This also protects healthy cells from

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.160
being attacked by the anti-cancer drug.
Remember the hydrophobic tails and

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.450
hydrophilic ends of the soap molecule?
Nature uses similar building blocks

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.010
called phospholipids in the outer
membrane of each one of your cells. These

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.300
building blocks contain one hydrophilic
end attached to two hydrophobic tails.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.459
These pieces self-assemble into a
bilayer, sort of like a nanoscale ice

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:47.560
cream sandwich. The center "ice cream" part
of the bilayer maximizes hydrophobic

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.550
interactions between the long tails and
positions of the hydrophilic end "cookies"

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:57.010
in contact with watery environments
inside or outside the cell. The formation

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.700
of this phospholipid bilayer keeps
important cellular components like the

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.330
nucleus and cytoplasm where they belong,
so that they can do what they're

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.350
supposed to do where it's supposed to
happen. This is one of the many

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:12.100
interacting bits and pieces that come
together to make what's arguably the

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:18.209
most beautiful and intricate example of
self-assembly in nature: You

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.510
 

